.TH I2C 3
.SH NAME
i2c \- I2C buses, as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #J /dev

.BI /dev/i2c N ctl
.BI /dev/i2c N data
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I I2c
is a polled master with 7-bit addresses. Which buses it serves is the
board's:
.TP
Raspberry Pi 5
bus 0 on GPIO 0 and 1, bus 1 on GPIO 2 and 3, on the header.
The pins become I2C's at the first open.
.TP
Raspberry Pi 4
bus 0 only: BSC0 on GPIO 44 and 45, the bus on the two ribbon
connectors (the camera's and the display's), at 400 kHz.
.PP
Writing the control file sets:
.TP
.BI addr " n"
the device to talk to.
.TP
.BI subaddress " n"
that each transfer starts with the file offset, in
.I n
bytes (0 to 4), high first, and that a read then sends it, and reads
after a repeated start (the Pi 5) or a stop (the Pi 4): how most
register and EEPROM devices are read.
.TP
.BI speed " n"
100000 or 400000 (the Pi 5; the Pi 4's bus is fixed at 400 kHz).
.PP
Reading it returns those settings.
Reads and writes of the data file are one transfer each with the device at
.IR addr ,
of at most 4096 bytes.
.PP
An address nothing answers gives the error
.BR "no device" ,
which is how to scan a bus.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/bcm2712/devi2c.c
(the Pi 5)
.br
.B /os/bcm2711/devi2c.c
(the Pi 4)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR camera (3),
.IR gpio (3),
.IR spi (3)
.SH BUGS
On the Pi 4 the VideoCore polls a DSI panel's touch controller on the
same bus, and there is no way to take turns. With a panel attached a
transfer can fail, and a read or a write can be silently wrong: a
program that matters reads its writes back.
